C1       THE THREE A5s, SEPARATED -- only one of them is a bit at all
         (a) THE_FRAMEWORK's own table row: 'the first mixed closure is torsion-free
             -- forces the word to be MIXED, aAbB not aA'.  That is a CONSTRAINT on
             the word, not a binary choice.  NOT a Z/2.
         (b) xB012's reading: 'the manifold restriction' -- the group is torsion-free.
             m004: a manifold (torsion-free group) -- H_1 = Z
             m003: a manifold (torsion-free group) -- H_1 = Z/5 + Z
             BOTH SISTERS ARE MANIFOLDS, so (b) CANNOT distinguish them.  NOT the bit.
         (c) H_1 torsion-free = KNOT-NESS = the m003/m004 bit.
             m004 H_1 = Z   m003 H_1 = Z/5 + Z   -> separates: True
C1 PASS  ONLY READING (c) IS A Z/2.  Path C's question presupposes (c), and the
         record uses 'A5' for all three.  A TERMINOLOGY HAZARD, named not silently
         resolved: this arc means A5(c) throughout.

C2       DOES A7 CHANGE THE OBJECT?  b++LR versus b++RL
         vol equal: True   H_1 equal: True (Z)   CS equal: True
         ISOMETRIC: True
         and the monodromies are CONJUGATE in SL(2,Z) via the record-swap P:
           P (LR) P^-1 = RL : True   (B979's statement, re-derived)
C2 PASS  A7 DOES NOT CHANGE THE OBJECT.  Every class invariant is
         blind to it, exactly as B979 says.

C3       DOES A5(c) CHANGE THE OBJECT?  b++LR versus b+-LR
         b++LR = m004: vol 2.0298832128  H_1 Z  CS -0.000000
         b+-LR = m003: vol 2.0298832128  H_1 Z/5 + Z  CS 0.250000
         SAME VOLUME: True   ISOMETRIC: False
C3 PASS  A5(c) CHANGES THE OBJECT -- same volume, DIFFERENT
         manifold, and H_1 sees it where the volume does not.

C4       THE DECISIVE COMPARISON, against all four same-ness criteria
         criterion 1, EQUAL AS MAPS: A7 fixes the homeomorphism type (C2); A5(c)
           does not (C3).  A map that fixes everything is not a map that changes
           something.  NOT EQUAL.
         criterion 2, SAME SUBGROUP: they act on different slots -- A7 on the WORD,
           A5(c) on the SIGN PREFIX -- and C7 measures the group they generate.
         criterion 3, SAME ACTION ON A NAMED INVARIANT: C5 measures it, and they
           disagree on every invariant tested.
         criterion 4, CONJUGATE: conjugate involutions have conjugate fixed sets;
           A7 fixes EVERY bundle up to isometry while A5(c) fixes NONE, so their
           fixed sets differ in size and they cannot be conjugate.
C4 PASS  THE A5 BIT AND THE A7 BIT ARE NOT THE SAME Z/2, under all four criteria.

C5       WHAT EACH BIT DOES TO THREE INVARIANTS -- the sharp form
         words tested: 240
         A7 (letter swap) NEGATES CS:            240 of 240   (B128 M-C)
         A5(c) (sign prefix) SHIFTS CS BY 1/4:   240 of 240   (xB015 K6)
         ON m004 ITSELF, where CS = 0:
           A7:    CS -> -CS = 0.  ACTS TRIVIALLY.
           A5(c): CS -> CS + 1/4 = 1/4.  ACTS NON-TRIVIALLY.
         and on B979's BASED invariant, the Mobius fixed-point polynomial:
           LR: t**2 + t - 1   roots [-1/2 + sqrt(5)/2, -sqrt(5)/2 - 1/2]
           RL: t**2 - t - 1   roots [1/2 - sqrt(5)/2, 1/2 + sqrt(5)/2]
           A7 MOVES this polynomial (B979, re-derived).  A5(c) does NOT touch the
           word at all, so it leaves the polynomial FIXED.
C5 PASS  THE SHARP FORM: the two bits act on CS by DIFFERENT
         group operations -- A7 by NEGATION, A5(c) by TRANSLATION BY 1/4.  On the
         2-torsion value CS = 0 that A6 selects, negation is the IDENTITY and
         translation is not.  THE A7 BIT DIES ON m004's OWN ZERO; THE A5 BIT DOES NOT.

C6       C versus P ON THE MINIMAL WORD -- the cell the seal could not call
         B1083 types the torsor as K_4 = <C (letter swap), P (reversal)>.
         on LR:  C(LR) = RL   P(LR) = RL   AGREE: True
         over all mixed words of length 2-8: agree 30, differ 464
         FIRST word where they differ: ('RRL', 'LLR', 'LRR')
         => THE TWO TORSOR BITS COINCIDE ON THE MINIMAL WORD AND SEPARATE ABOVE IT.
            The K_4 COLLAPSES TO A SINGLE Z/2 EXACTLY AT THE OBJECT A6 SELECTS --
            which is WHY A7 is 'one bit' and not two.  A6's minimality is what
            makes the torsor look one-dimensional.
         HONEST SCOPE: this is a statement about the WORD, the axis this arc varies.
         It does NOT say the two bits are the same in general -- they are not, above
         length 2 -- and it is NOT a selection principle.
C6 PASS  the collapse is real and it is A6's doing.

C7       THE GROUP GENERATED, acting on the family {b±±W}
         A5(c): (s, W) -> (-s, W)      [flips the sign prefix, fixes the word]
         A7:    (s, W) -> ( s, C(W))   [swaps letters, fixes the prefix]
         They act on DISJOINT coordinates of the pair (prefix, word), so they
         COMMUTE, each is an involution, and neither is the identity ->
         <A5(c), A7> = Z/2 x Z/2 = K_4, NOT Z/2.
         exhibited on W = RRL: the orbit has 4 elements -> [('+', 'LLR'), ('+', 'RRL'), ('-', 'LLR'), ('-', 'RRL')]
C7 PASS  INDEPENDENT.  The two bits generate a K_4 of their own,
         distinct from B1083's torsor K_4 = <C, P>, which lives entirely inside the
         WORD coordinate.

C8       THE VERDICT, with every axis held fixed NAMED
         ANSWER: NO. The A5 bit and the A7 bit are NOT the same Z/2, under all four
         same-ness criteria the seal declared.
         AND THE INTERESTING PART IS NOT THAT THEY DIFFER, BUT WHERE EACH IS VISIBLE:
           A7  is invisible to every CLASS invariant (it fixes the manifold) and
               visible only to a BASED invariant -- B979's Mobius fixed-point
               polynomial.  On CS it acts by NEGATION, so it DIES on m004's zero.
           A5(c) is invisible to the CHARACTER VARIETY (xB007) and visible in the
               k-COUPLING (xB015 K6).  On CS it acts by TRANSLATION BY 1/4, so it
               SURVIVES on m004's zero.
         TWO BITS, EACH INVISIBLE TO THE LAYER THE OTHER LIVES ON.
         AND C6's FINDING, which the seal could not call: the torsor's OWN two bits
           (C and P) COINCIDE on the minimal word and separate only above it -- so
           the K_4 collapses to one Z/2 exactly at the object A6 selects, which is
           WHY A7 reads as 'one bit'.  A6's minimality is doing that work.

         AXES HELD FIXED, NAMED AS THE RULE REQUIRES:
           - the monodromy's ambient group: SL(2,Z).  GL(2,Z) (det -1, the Breath
             pulse of B1083's M) IS NOT VARIED HERE.
           - the bundles: b++ and b+- only; b-+ and b-- do not parse in SnapPy for
             these words and were NOT tested.
           - word length <= 8 for C5/C6.
           - only ONE reading of A5 is carried through (c); (a) and (b) are named in
             C1 and then set aside, WITH the reason.

